9864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cypress-io/cypress/commit/d82e720e9ede0042feb87a4626bfae34f712ab59","node_id":"C_kwDOAeKht9oAKGQ4MmU3MjBlOWVkZTAwNDJmZWI4N2E0NjI2YmZhZTM0ZjcxMmFiNTk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cypress-io/cypress/commit/b95971bef1813cedecfb2397b300f4ded5eadfc1","sha":"b95971bef1813cedecfb2397b300f4ded5eadfc1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cypress-io/cypress/commits/b95971bef1813cedecfb2397b300f4ded5eadfc1"}],"sha":"d82e720e9ede0042feb87a4626bfae34f712ab59","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cypress-io/cypress/commits/d82e720e9ede0042feb87a4626bfae34f712ab59"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/142877?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/cacieprins","id":142877,"login":"cacieprins","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0Mjg3Nw==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cacieprins","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cypress-io/cypress/commits/b95971bef1813cedecfb2397b300f4ded5eadfc1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T18:05:05Z","email":"cacieprins@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Cacie Prins"},"comment_count":1,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T18:05:05Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: present clientCertificates on the browser network path (#34904)\n\n* fix: present clientCertificates on the browser network path (#34807)\n\nOn the browser network path Chrome, Chromium and Edge perform their own\nDNS, TCP and TLS, and CDP exposes no way to hand the browser a client\ncertificate. Only the Cypress Node process holds the private key, so\nmutual TLS could not be negotiated for any request the browser issued.\n`cy.request()` and the visited document kept working because Node fetches\nthose itself, which is why the failure only showed up on subresources.\n\nWhen `clientCertificates` is configured and that path is active, each\nconfigured origin is now steered at a local listener with\n`--host-resolver-rules`. The listener terminates the browser''s TLS with a\ncertificate minted from the existing HTTPS proxy CA, performs the real\nmutual-TLS handshake to the origin from Node, offers the browser exactly\nthe protocol the origin selected, and then pipes bytes. Nothing above TLS\nis parsed, so HTTP/2 survives end to end and CDP interception, cy.intercept\nand Test Replay are unaffected. Runs with no client certificate configured\nare untouched.\n\nA connection carries one client certificate, chosen before any path is\nknown, so entries that differ only by path collapse onto one origin and\nconflicting material for a single origin is now reported at startup.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: keep the mTLS bridge alive across specs and destroy it cleanly\n\nThe bridge was rebuilt on every launch, but its ports reach the browser as\n`--host-resolver-rules`, which Chrome reads once at launch. A later spec\nreuses that browser instead of relaunching it, so every spec after the\nfirst was steered at a port that had just been closed: the request either\nstalled or reached the origin with no certificate. The bridge is now\ncreated once per project, so the ports the browser was told about stay\nvalid for as long as that browser runs. A second spec against a second\norigin covers it — reusing the first origin can succeed on a connection\nthe browser already holds open, which hides the stale rule.\n\n`close()` used `server.close()`, which leaves established sockets running\nand only resolves once they end, so a kept-alive HTTP/2 session could\nstall teardown. It now uses `allowDestroy`, matching the HTTPS proxy.\n\nThe upstream dial resolved its proxy through two calls that could\ndisagree: `shouldProxyForUrl` honors `HTTP_PROXY_TARGET_FOR_ORIGIN_REQUESTS`\nwhile `getProxyForUrl` does not, so with that override set and no\n`HTTP_PROXY` the dial fell through to a direct connection. Both are\nreplaced by `getProxyOrTargetOverrideForUrl`, now exported from\n`@packages/network`, which also drops an undeclared `proxy-from-env`\ndependency that Knip flagged.\n\nAlso stop sending an IP literal as `servername` — browsers send no SNI for\nIP origins and Node deprecated the practice — and destroy the upstream\nsocket when the browser socket closes mid-handshake.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: merge the changelog entry into the existing Bugfixes section\n\nMerging develop left this release with two `**Bugfixes:**` headers and no\nblank line before `**Performance:**`, both of which the changelog parser\nrejects. The entry moves into the section that already exists, ahead of\nthe timer fix since a hung run is the more disruptive regression.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: release the origin connection when the browser leaves mid-handshake\n\nA ''close'' listener registered after the upstream handshake resolves cannot\nhear a close that already happened, so a browser that goes away while the\norigin handshake is still running left an established connection to the\norigin with nothing holding it. The condition is now recorded before the\nhandshake starts and checked once it returns.\n\nThe paths after this point usually clean up on their own, which is why no\nregression test accompanies this: the window needs the server side of the\nbrowser socket to have finished closing before the listener is attached,\nand that is not reachable through the seams this class exposes. A test that\npassed with and without the guard would only look like coverage.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: stop discarding browser records sent during the origin handshake\n\nRemoving the last ''data'' listener does not take a socket out of flowing\nmode, so bytes that arrived between reading the ClientHello and pausing\nwere read off the socket and dropped. TLS 1.3 sends a change_cipher_spec\nimmediately after the ClientHello, and the window is the whole origin\nhandshake — wide against a remote origin, and narrow enough on loopback\nthat the tests never saw it. Verified directly: a second record written\n50ms after the first never survived the replay, and does once the socket\nis paused in the same tick as the listener removal.\n\nAn IPv6 literal also reached `net.connect` and `net.isIP` still bracketed,\nsince that is the form `URL.hostname` yields. Both now see it bare, while\nthe URL used for proxy resolution keeps the brackets it needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: bypass the upstream proxy for origins steered at the mTLS bridge\n\nChromium picks the proxy from a URL''s host before resolving it, so with\nHTTP_PROXY set it sent a bridged origin to the proxy by name and the\nresolver rule never fired. Measured against Chrome with a stand-in proxy:\nthe proxy received CONNECT for the origin three times and the listener\nreceived nothing; adding the origin to the bypass list moved every one of\nthose connections to the listener. The bridge still reaches the origin\nthrough the proxy itself, since it dials with CONNECT.\n\n`translateEgressPolicyToLaunchOpts` already did this for `hosts`, for the\nsame reason, so the bridged origins now go through the same path.\n\nAlso covers the real upstream dial, which was untested because the bridge\nspec injects a fake: the CONNECT handshake, a status line split across\nsegments, a non-200 answer, and a proxy that closes without answering.\n\nAn entry whose material is missing now raises an error rather than being\ndropped, so a configured certificate cannot be accepted and then quietly\nnot presented, and the conflict error names `forceHttp1: true` as the way\nto keep per-path entries.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: bound the ClientHello scan and narrow what the bridge steers\n\nA peer can declare a TLS record longer than the vectors inside it\ndescribe. The scan read past the end, got `undefined`, and threw\n`ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` from the next offset read — inside a socket data\nhandler, so anything that connected to a listener and sent a bad record\ntook the whole run down rather than just its own connection. Each vector\nis bounded against the record now, and an overrun is simply not a\nClientHello.\n\nThe proxy bypass covered a bridged host on every port and scheme, but only\n`host:port` is steered. With direct egress blocked, the host''s other ports\nwere taken off the proxy and dialed directly, failing where they used to\nwork, and a `url: ''*''` entry took every request off the proxy while\nbridging only 443. The bypass now carries the port.\n\nResolver rules are emitted most specific first. Chromium applies the first\nmatch and a wildcard also matches a host an exact entry names, so a\ncatch-all ahead of an override steered that origin to the wrong listener\nand presented the wrong certificate. The two are different origins, so the\nconflict check never saw them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: pin the ALPN mirror and stop a stalled origin leaking the dial\n\nThe two tests that claimed to pin the ALPN mirror did not. Both used an\norigin speaking h2 and http/1.1, where offering the browser its own list\nand offering it the origin''s choice produce the same answer, so replacing\nthe mirror with the browser''s list left all 32 tests passing. The case\nthat matters is an origin that downgrades — common for mutual TLS, since\nHTTP/2 forbids TLS renegotiation — where the browser would be told h2 and\nthen speak h2 frames at an http/1.1 origin. Two origins that can show the\ndifference now cover it, asserting the negotiated protocol rather than a\nresponse body, and they fail if the mirror is removed.\n\nAn origin or CONNECT proxy that completes TCP and then goes silent left\nthe dial pending forever. Everything that cleans up is registered after\nthat await, so the socket and the promise leaked for the life of the\nprocess while the browser retried. The dial now has a deadline and takes\nthe socket down with it.\n\nA failed mint was cached permanently, so one transient file error poisoned\nthat origin''s certificate for the rest of the project. A partial `listen()`\nfailure stranded the listeners that had already bound. Two overlapping\nlaunches could each build a bridge and orphan one still bound.\n\nAlso covers the CONNECT path completing a real handshake, which no test\nreached before: every existing case killed the tunnel before TLS started.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: clear the upstream deadline once the connection is established\n\nThe deadline timer added in 63a6371594 was never cleared, so thirty\nseconds after a successful dial it destroyed the socket it had been\nguarding — by then the live TLS connection. Every bridged connection\nwould have been torn down mid-session, dropping the HTTP/2 session and\nany request in flight on it. The suite never saw it because every run\nfinishes well inside the deadline; the regression test takes a deadline\nargument so it can.\n\nA rejected bridge creation was also cached for the life of the project,\nso a transient bind or CA failure would be replayed to every later launch\nand the run could never recover.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b3296c47aa2f5546fc96c684d18dc73553bb9d09","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cypress-io/cypress/git/trees/b3296c47aa2f5546fc96c684d18dc73553bb9d09"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cypress-io/cypress/git/commits/b95971bef1813cedecfb2397b300f4ded5eadfc1","verification":{"payload":"tree b3296c47aa2f5546fc96c684d18dc73553bb9d09\nparent cc1ae4528804a7c295b549162283dcc1d8b3ee76\nauthor Cacie Prins <cacieprins@users.noreply.github.com> 1791396305 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791396305 -0400\n\nfix: present clientCertificates on the browser network path (#34904)\n\n* fix: present clientCertificates on the browser network path (#34807)\n\nOn the browser network path Chrome, Chromium and Edge perform their own\nDNS, TCP and TLS, and CDP exposes no way to hand the browser a client\ncertificate. Only the Cypress Node process holds the private key, so\nmutual TLS could not be negotiated for any request the browser issued.\n`cy.request()` and the visited document kept working because Node fetches\nthose itself, which is why the failure only showed up on subresources.\n\nWhen `clientCertificates` is configured and that path is active, each\nconfigured origin is now steered at a local listener with\n`--host-resolver-rules`. The listener terminates the browser''s TLS with a\ncertificate minted from the existing HTTPS proxy CA, performs the real\nmutual-TLS handshake to the origin from Node, offers the browser exactly\nthe protocol the origin selected, and then pipes bytes. Nothing above TLS\nis parsed, so HTTP/2 survives end to end and CDP interception, cy.intercept\nand Test Replay are unaffected. Runs with no client certificate configured\nare untouched.\n\nA connection carries one client certificate, chosen before any path is\nknown, so entries that differ only by path collapse onto one origin and\nconflicting material for a single origin is now reported at startup.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: keep the mTLS bridge alive across specs and destroy it cleanly\n\nThe bridge was rebuilt on every launch, but its ports reach the browser as\n`--host-resolver-rules`, which Chrome reads once at launch. A later spec\nreuses that browser instead of relaunching it, so every spec after the\nfirst was steered at a port that had just been closed: the request either\nstalled or reached the origin with no certificate. The bridge is now\ncreated once per project, so the ports the browser was told about stay\nvalid for as long as that browser runs. A second spec against a second\norigin covers it — reusing the first origin can succeed on a connection\nthe browser already holds open, which hides the stale rule.\n\n`close()` used `server.close()`, which leaves established sockets running\nand only resolves once they end, so a kept-alive HTTP/2 session could\nstall teardown. It now uses `allowDestroy`, matching the HTTPS proxy.\n\nThe upstream dial resolved its proxy through two calls that could\ndisagree: `shouldProxyForUrl` honors `HTTP_PROXY_TARGET_FOR_ORIGIN_REQUESTS`\nwhile `getProxyForUrl` does not, so with that override set and no\n`HTTP_PROXY` the dial fell through to a direct connection. Both are\nreplaced by `getProxyOrTargetOverrideForUrl`, now exported from\n`@packages/network`, which also drops an undeclared `proxy-from-env`\ndependency that Knip flagged.\n\nAlso stop sending an IP literal as `servername` — browsers send no SNI for\nIP origins and Node deprecated the practice — and destroy the upstream\nsocket when the browser socket closes mid-handshake.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: merge the changelog entry into the existing Bugfixes section\n\nMerging develop left this release with two `**Bugfixes:**` headers and no\nblank line before `**Performance:**`, both of which the changelog parser\nrejects. The entry moves into the section that already exists, ahead of\nthe timer fix since a hung run is the more disruptive regression.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: release the origin connection when the browser leaves mid-handshake\n\nA ''close'' listener registered after the upstream handshake resolves cannot\nhear a close that already happened, so a browser that goes away while the\norigin handshake is still running left an established connection to the\norigin with nothing holding it. The condition is now recorded before the\nhandshake starts and checked once it returns.\n\nThe paths after this point usually clean up on their own, which is why no\nregression test accompanies this: the window needs the server side of the\nbrowser socket to have finished closing before the listener is attached,\nand that is not reachable through the seams this class exposes. A test that\npassed with and without the guard would only look like coverage.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: stop discarding browser records sent during the origin handshake\n\nRemoving the last ''data'' listener does not take a socket out of flowing\nmode, so bytes that arrived between reading the ClientHello and pausing\nwere read off the socket and dropped. TLS 1.3 sends a change_cipher_spec\nimmediately after the ClientHello, and the window is the whole origin\nhandshake — wide against a remote origin, and narrow enough on loopback\nthat the tests never saw it. Verified directly: a second record written\n50ms after the first never survived the replay, and does once the socket\nis paused in the same tick as the listener removal.\n\nAn IPv6 literal also reached `net.connect` and `net.isIP` still bracketed,\nsince that is the form `URL.hostname` yields. Both now see it bare, while\nthe URL used for proxy resolution keeps the brackets it needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: bypass the upstream proxy for origins steered at the mTLS bridge\n\nChromium picks the proxy from a URL''s host before resolving it, so with\nHTTP_PROXY set it sent a bridged origin to the proxy by name and the\nresolver rule never fired. Measured against Chrome with a stand-in proxy:\nthe proxy received CONNECT for the origin three times and the listener\nreceived nothing; adding the origin to the bypass list moved every one of\nthose connections to the listener. The bridge still reaches the origin\nthrough the proxy itself, since it dials with CONNECT.\n\n`translateEgressPolicyToLaunchOpts` already did this for `hosts`, for the\nsame reason, so the bridged origins now go through the same path.\n\nAlso covers the real upstream dial, which was untested because the bridge\nspec injects a fake: the CONNECT handshake, a status line split across\nsegments, a non-200 answer, and a proxy that closes without answering.\n\nAn entry whose material is missing now raises an error rather than being\ndropped, so a configured certificate cannot be accepted and then quietly\nnot presented, and the conflict error names `forceHttp1: true` as the way\nto keep per-path entries.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: bound the ClientHello scan and narrow what the bridge steers\n\nA peer can declare a TLS record longer than the vectors inside it\ndescribe. The scan read past the end, got `undefined`, and threw\n`ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` from the next offset read — inside a socket data\nhandler, so anything that connected to a listener and sent a bad record\ntook the whole run down rather than just its own connection. Each vector\nis bounded against the record now, and an overrun is simply not a\nClientHello.\n\nThe proxy bypass covered a bridged host on every port and scheme, but only\n`host:port` is steered. With direct egress blocked, the host''s other ports\nwere taken off the proxy and dialed directly, failing where they used to\nwork, and a `url: ''*''` entry took every request off the proxy while\nbridging only 443. The bypass now carries the port.\n\nResolver rules are emitted most specific first. Chromium applies the first\nmatch and a wildcard also matches a host an exact entry names, so a\ncatch-all ahead of an override steered that origin to the wrong listener\nand presented the wrong certificate. The two are different origins, so the\nconflict check never saw them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: pin the ALPN mirror and stop a stalled origin leaking the dial\n\nThe two tests that claimed to pin the ALPN mirror did not. Both used an\norigin speaking h2 and http/1.1, where offering the browser its own list\nand offering it the origin''s choice produce the same answer, so replacing\nthe mirror with the browser''s list left all 32 tests passing. The case\nthat matters is an origin that downgrades — common for mutual TLS, since\nHTTP/2 forbids TLS renegotiation — where the browser would be told h2 and\nthen speak h2 frames at an http/1.1 origin. Two origins that can show the\ndifference now cover it, asserting the negotiated protocol rather than a\nresponse body, and they fail if the mirror is removed.\n\nAn origin or CONNECT proxy that completes TCP and then goes silent left\nthe dial pending forever. Everything that cleans up is registered after\nthat await, so the socket and the promise leaked for the life of the\nprocess while the browser retried. The dial now has a deadline and takes\nthe socket down with it.\n\nA failed mint was cached permanently, so one transient file error poisoned\nthat origin''s certificate for the rest of the project. A partial `listen()`\nfailure stranded the listeners that had already bound. Two overlapping\nlaunches could each build a bridge and orphan one still bound.\n\nAlso covers the CONNECT path completing a real handshake, which no test\nreached before: every existing case killed the tunnel before TLS started.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: clear the upstream deadline once the connection is established\n\nThe deadline timer added in 63a6371594 was never cleared, so thirty\nseconds after a successful dial it destroyed the socket it had been\nguarding — by then the live TLS connection. Every bridged connection\nwould have been torn down mid-session, dropping the HTTP/2 session and\nany request in flight on it. The suite never saw it because every run\nfinishes well inside the deadline; the regression test takes a deadline\nargument so it can.\n\nA rejected bridge creation was also cached for the life of the project,\nso a transient bind or CA failure would be replayed to every later launch\nand the run could never recover.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxonRCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAm2AQAHZk9XIjedTsjvU7Z4Gs3+pO\n9mIC6JJht1ID0JUaqP0z0dIo9tlkreDt81tt69n0oOCbX5Z9JoRgnG80ifkG0k1t\nzgKzQmiYMTqBQuC48bBjUN1nQNTFK4XBgKZr9IFz+McjE57XekwNG6VxDn2bbISt\nJcT8T51e8O9UORf2IomtSFmRsoz5ev672Qc4TFlvV6rfRESNrc1yw/tcNTofAlvb\nwbz9bAyEamnxKw4+73C2Azr2S+gnl//w6E0oEVCJj